⍝ Quad_PNG.tc
⍝ ----------------------------------

⍝@ test3.

       ]NEXTFILE no-⎕PNG        ⍝ done if ⎕PNG is not available
       ]NEXTFILE no-GTK3        ⍝ done if GTK is not available

      ⍝ create a 4×5  random image, RGB, 16-bit precision
      ⍝
      IMAGE←(1.0× ? 3 100 200⍴$10000)÷$10000

      HANDLE←⎕PNG IMAGE        ⍝ display IMAGE

      HANDLE = ⎕PNG HANDLE     ⍝ close the display windows
1

      '/tmp/xxx' ⎕PNG IMAGE    ⍝ write IMAGE to file /tmp/xxx

      IMAGE1←⎕PNG '/tmp/xxx'   ⍝ read file back

      (⍴IMAGE) ≡ ⍴IMAGE1
1

      ⍝ GNU APL uses floating point pixels in the range [0.0 - 1.0]. PNG uses
      ⍝ integer values instead. Converting APL pixels to PNG pixels will
      ⍝ therefore cause small rounding errors. For that reason we cannot
      ⍝ IMAGE ≡ IMAGE1 but instead use the maximum difference between
      ⍝ IMAGE and IMAGE1
      ⍝
      ⊢MAX_DIFF←⌈/∣,IMAGE-IMAGE1   ⍝ max. pixel difference
²
      MAX_DIFF<0.1
1

      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs12.md #10, 2026-08-06):
      ⍝ valid_type_and_bits() computed "1 << bit_depth" directly on a
      ⍝ user-supplied, unchecked bit_depth; the shift is UB outside
      ⍝ [0, 31] and, on x86 (which masks the shift count to 5 bits),
      ⍝ made out-of-range depths like 33 and 40 wrongly pass validation
      ⍝ (33 mod 32 == 1, a genuinely valid depth's bit). Both are
      ⍝ rejected now via an explicit bit_depth<=16 range check before
      ⍝ the shift; a genuinely valid depth (8, used throughout this
      ⍝ file's own IMAGE writes above) is unaffected.
      ⍝
      B←1 4 4⍴16⍴0.5
      ('/tmp/bugs12_10.png' 33) ⎕PNG B
DOMAIN ERROR+
      '/tmp/bugs12_10.png' 33 ⎕PNG B
      ^                            ^
)SIC

      ('/tmp/bugs12_10.png' 40) ⎕PNG B
DOMAIN ERROR+
      '/tmp/bugs12_10.png' 40 ⎕PNG B
      ^                            ^
)SIC

      )ERASE B

      )ERASE IMAGE IMAGE1 HANDLE MAX_DIFF

      )HOST rm /tmp/xxx

0 

      )CHECK
OK      - no stale functions
OK      - no stale values
OK      - no stale indices
OK      - no duplicate parents

